---
// IEM (in the canal) vs over-ear (around the ear) — schematic placement. Static teaching diagram for
// the IEMs-vs-over-ear article.
---

<figure class="fig">
  <div class="figscroll"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 280" role="img" aria-label="Two schematics: an in-ear monitor sealing the ear canal, and an over-ear cup enclosing the whole ear at a distance.">
    <defs>
      <marker id="ep-ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><path d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z" fill="#FF9E2C"></path></marker>
    </defs>

    <!-- ===== IEM panel ===== -->
    <g>
      <!-- ear (pinna) + canal -->
      <ellipse cx="230" cy="120" rx="30" ry="44" fill="#1b1d20" stroke="#868d92" stroke-width="1.6"></ellipse>
      <path d="M212 120 L150 120" stroke="#34383d" stroke-width="14" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
      <path d="M212 120 L152 120" stroke="#0f1012" stroke-width="9" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
      <!-- IEM tip in the canal + body outside -->
      <ellipse cx="200" cy="120" rx="16" ry="9" fill="#26292d" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="1.8"></ellipse>
      <path d="M186 111 q-22 -6 -34 3 q-6 6 2 13 q14 6 32 2 z" fill="#26292d" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="1.6"></path>
      <!-- sound into the canal -->
      <line x1="212" y1="120" x2="248" y2="120" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ep-ar)"></line>
      <text x="190" y="212" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" letter-spacing="0.08em" fill="#e8ebed">IEM</text>
      <text x="190" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="10.5" fill="#565b60">seals the canal · isolates</text>
    </g>

    <!-- ===== over-ear panel ===== -->
    <g>
      <ellipse cx="565" cy="120" rx="30" ry="44" fill="#1b1d20" stroke="#868d92" stroke-width="1.6"></ellipse>
      <path d="M547 120 L485 120" stroke="#34383d" stroke-width="14" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
      <path d="M547 120 L487 120" stroke="#0f1012" stroke-width="9" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
      <!-- cup enclosing the whole ear, at a distance -->
      <path d="M636 58 A72 72 0 0 1 636 182" fill="none" stroke="#e8ebed" stroke-width="2.4"></path>
      <path d="M628 74 A56 56 0 0 1 628 166" fill="none" stroke="#34383d" stroke-width="10"></path>
      <!-- driver + sound across the gap to the ear -->
      <line x1="620" y1="120" x2="622" y2="120" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="12" stroke-linecap="round"></line>
      <line x1="612" y1="120" x2="600" y2="120" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ep-ar)"></line>
      <line x1="608" y1="102" x2="596" y2="106" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ep-ar)"></line>
      <line x1="608" y1="138" x2="596" y2="134" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#ep-ar)"></line>
      <text x="565" y="212" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="13" letter-spacing="0.08em" fill="#e8ebed">OVER-EAR</text>
      <text x="565" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="10.5" fill="#565b60">around the ear · roomier</text>
    </g>
  </svg></div>
  <figcaption>
    Where the driver sits changes everything. An <em>IEM</em> seals in the ear canal &mdash; tiny, isolating,
    fit-critical. An <em>over-ear</em> plays from a distance around the whole ear &mdash; roomier and comfier, less
    isolating.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig { margin: 28px 0 0; background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius); padding: 18px 18px 14px; }
  /* Fixed 760-unit grid: scaling to a narrow column scales the labels with it, so hold a
     legible floor and let the figure scroll sideways instead of shrinking the type. */
  .fig svg { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; min-width: 600px; }
  .fig .figscroll { overflow-x: auto; overscroll-behavior-x: contain; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .fig svg { min-width: 0; }
    .fig .figscroll { overflow-x: visible; }
  }
  .fig figcaption { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--muted-2); font-family: var(--mono); }
  .fig figcaption em { color: var(--muted); font-style: italic; }
</style>
